# Target C-II — fully rhombic pentagons are exact two-blockers in the train-crossing graph

**Date:** 2026-09-15\
**Status:** proof-only exact structural theorem\
**Scope:** canonical fully force-visible full-core Type-II branch; finite statement once a fully rhombic pentagonal face exists\
**Dependencies:** strict convexity/unit separation of bounded pentagonal contact faces; finite stopped-train crossing geometry; bounded-face endpoint fan\
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Let `F` be a fully rhombic bounded pentagonal face.  Its five unit boundary contacts
belong to five distinct non-singleton strict-rhombus stopped trains; denote this set by

\[
I(F)=\{\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_5\}.
\]

The key local fact is purely metric:

\[
\boxed{
\text{no projective arc of length }\pi/3\text{ contains four side directions of }F.
}
\tag{FB.1}
\]

A strict-rhombus crossing between two complete trains requires projective direction
separation strictly greater than `pi/3`.  For one fixed train direction, the set of
projective directions that can satisfy this necessary crossing condition is one open
arc of length exactly `pi/3`.  Therefore (FB.1) implies

\[
\boxed{
\text{every train }\sigma\notin I(F)
\text{ is noncrossing with at least two members of }I(F).
}
\tag{FB.2}
\]

Equivalently, every external train crosses at most three of the five flower trains.
This gives an exact degree inequality in the strict-rhombus train-crossing graph
`G_R` with `T_R` vertices and `R_4` edges:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{\tau\in I(F)}d_R(\tau)\le3(T_R-5).
}
\tag{FB.3}
\]

If `M` fully rhombic pentagons are present and

\[
s_\tau:=\#\{F:\tau\in I(F),\ F\text{ fully rhombic pentagon}\}\in\{0,1,2\},
\]

then summing (FB.3) gives the weighted endpoint-degree bound

\[
\boxed{
\sum_\tau s_\tau d_R(\tau)
\le3M(T_R-5),
\qquad
\sum_\tau s_\tau=5M.
}
\tag{FB.4}
\]

Thus the average crossing degree seen from a full-flower terminal occurrence is at
most

\[
\boxed{{3\over5}(T_R-5).}
\tag{FB.5}
\]

There is also a global blocker consequence.  Any train `sigma` is incident to at most
two fully rhombic pentagons.  For every other full flower, (FB.2) supplies two blocker
terminal occurrences; one blocker train can account for at most its two endpoints.
Hence

\[
\boxed{
\bar d_R(\sigma)\ge M-2
\qquad\text{for every strict-rhombus train }\sigma,
}
\tag{FB.6}
\]

where `bar d_R` is nondegree among the other strict-rhombus trains.  Consequently

\[
\boxed{
{T_R\choose2}-R_4\ge {T_R(M-2)\over2}.
}
\tag{FB.7}
\]

The useful new information is not another scalar Mantel floor: the modern full-flower
branch already has a much larger aggregate Mantel deficit.  The gain is localization.
Equations (FB.3)--(FB.5) force that deficit to be visible on the actual trains
terminating at full pentagonal flowers, with no phase-purity or near-lattice
assumption.  This is the missing interface between the general full-flower normal form
and phase-resolved train allocation arguments such as dominant-pentagon splicing.

---

## 1. Strict convex unit pentagons have turns below `2pi/3`

Orient the boundary of `F` counterclockwise and let its successive unit side vectors be

\[
e_1,\ldots,e_5,
\qquad |e_i|=1,
\qquad \sum_i e_i=0.
\]

Let `g_i in (0,pi)` be the positive turning angle from `e_i` to `e_{i+1}`.  The
corresponding two-edge diagonal has length

\[
|e_i+e_{i+1}|=2\cos(g_i/2).
\]

It joins two nonconsecutive source vertices, so unit separation and the fact that the
pentagon has no diagonal contact give

\[
|e_i+e_{i+1}|>1.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{0<g_i<2\pi/3\quad(1\le i\le5).}
\tag{FB.8}
\]

This is exactly the strict-turn fact used below.

---

## 2. Four projective side directions cannot fit in a `pi/3` arc

Assume for contradiction that at least four projective side directions lie in a
closed projective arc `A` of width

\[
w\le\pi/3.
\]

Rotate coordinates so the midpoint axis of `A` is horizontal.  Every oriented side
whose projective direction lies in `A` lies either in the lifted cone `A_+` around the
positive horizontal axis or in the antipodal cone `A_-`.  Each cone has angular width
at most `pi/3`, so every such side has horizontal projection of absolute value at
least

\[
c:=\cos(w/2)\ge\cos(\pi/6)={\sqrt3\over2}.
\tag{FB.9}
\]

First suppose all five projective directions lie in `A`.  Among five oriented vectors,
one of the two cones contains at least three sides and the other at most two.  Taking
the sign of the majority cone positive, the total horizontal projection has magnitude
at least

\[
3c-2>0,
\]

contradicting `sum e_i=0`.

Now suppose exactly four side directions lie in `A`.  Count how their oriented lifts
split between `A_+` and `A_-`.

### 2.1 A `4+0` or `3+1` split cannot close

For a `4+0` split, the four selected sides have total horizontal projection at least
`4c`; the fifth side contributes at worst `-1`, so the total is positive.

For a `3+1` split, orient the majority cone positively.  The three positive sides
contribute at least `3c`; the one selected negative side and the fifth arbitrary unit
side contribute at worst `-1` each.  Hence the total horizontal projection is at least

\[
3c-2\ge {3\sqrt3\over2}-2>0,
\]

again contradicting closure.  The reflected `1+3` and `0+4` cases are identical.

### 2.2 A `2+2` split contradicts the strict turn bound

It remains that two selected oriented sides lie in `A_+` and two in `A_-`.  On the
oriented direction circle, the two lifted cones are antipodal and each has width at
most `w`.  The two complementary gaps between them therefore each have angular length
at least

\[
\pi-w\ge2\pi/3.
\tag{FB.10}
\]

The fifth side has projective direction outside `A`, so its oriented direction lies
outside `A_+ union A_-`.  It can lie in only one of the two complementary gaps.  The
other gap remains a gap between two consecutive oriented side directions of the
pentagon and has size at least `2pi/3`, contradicting (FB.8).

All cases are impossible.  This proves (FB.1).

The constant is sharp at the combinatorial level: the exact pentagon lattice-phase
regression has three projective side directions collapsing into one phase while the
other two approach the two adjacent triangular-lattice phases.  Thus `three` in
(FB.1) cannot be replaced by `two` without additional global-minimality input.

---

## 3. Every external train has two blockers at one full flower

Fix a complete strict-rhombus train `sigma` with projective contact direction `phi`.
On the projective direction circle of circumference `pi`, define

\[
C_\phi:=\{\psi:d_{proj}(\phi,\psi)>\pi/3\}.
\]

Because `d_proj<=pi/2`, `C_phi` is one open arc of length exactly `pi/3`.
A strict unit-rhombus crossing of `sigma` with another train can occur only if that
other train's direction lies in `C_phi`.

If `sigma notin I(F)`, all five flower trains are distinct from `sigma`.  By (FB.1),
at most three of their projective directions lie in `C_phi`.  Therefore at least two
members of `I(F)` cannot cross `sigma`.  This proves (FB.2).

If `sigma in I(F)`, the bounded-face endpoint fan says `sigma` crosses none of the
other four trains in `I(F)` anyway.

---

## 4. Degree localization on one flower

Count incidences

\[
(\sigma,\tau),
\qquad
\sigma\notin I(F),\quad
\tau\in I(F),\quad
\sigma\pitchfork\tau.
\]

There are `T_R-5` choices for `sigma`, and by (FB.2) at most three choices for a
crossing `tau`.  Since the five trains in `I(F)` are pairwise noncrossing by the
bounded-face endpoint fan, this incidence count is exactly

\[
\sum_{\tau\in I(F)}d_R(\tau).
\]

Hence

\[
\sum_{\tau\in I(F)}d_R(\tau)
\le3(T_R-5),
\]

which is (FB.3).

Summing over all `M` fully rhombic pentagons counts `d_R(tau)` exactly `s_tau` times.
Every complete train has two terminal occurrences, so `s_tau<=2`, while the five
terminal occurrences at each flower give

\[
\sum_\tau s_\tau=5M.
\]

This proves (FB.4)--(FB.5).

---

## 5. Every train has linearly many blockers when flowers are linear

Fix one strict-rhombus train `sigma`.  It can terminate on at most two fully rhombic
pentagons.  For each of the remaining at least `M-2` full flowers, (FB.2) supplies at
least two train-terminal blocker occurrences belonging to trains nonadjacent to
`sigma` in `G_R`.

A fixed blocker train has only two terminal occurrences, so it can be supplied by at
most two different flowers.  Therefore the at least `2(M-2)` blocker occurrences use
at least `M-2` distinct blocker trains.  Thus

\[
\bar d_R(\sigma)\ge M-2.
\]

Summing this nondegree floor over all `T_R` vertices and dividing by two proves
(FB.7).

A slightly more informative finite form is

\[
\boxed{
R_4\le {T_R(T_R-M+1)\over2}.
}
\tag{FB.11}
\]

No endpoint-simplicity theorem between two bounded faces is needed for this argument;
the two-terminal capacity of one complete train is enough.

---

## 6. Relation to the live full-flower branch

The modern singleton/full-flower theorem gives a linear `M` in its full-flower branch,
and its scope extensions push that alternative far above the current O--O path
endpoint.  The existing theorem records only

1. the number of flower-terminal trains;
2. a global train-isoperimetric slack; and
3. the resulting aggregate Mantel deficit.

The blocker theorem adds directional localization:

```text
one full flower
    -> five distinct strict-rhombus train terminals
    -> every external train misses at least two of them
    -> sum of the five crossing degrees <= 3(T_R-5);

M full flowers
    -> weighted flower-endpoint average degree <= 3(T_R-5)/5
    -> every train has at least M-2 flower-supplied nonneighbors.
```

This does not by itself close C-II.  In the current full-flower branch the already
known aggregate Mantel deficit can be numerically larger than the coarse scalar
consequence (FB.7).  The new content is where the missing crossings live.  A useful
next step is to combine (FB.4) with the exact atomic-orbit allocation: if too many of
the `5M` flower terminals lie in the dominant orbit, their localized degree loss can
be charged directly against dominant crossing mass; if many lie outside it, the same
terminal occurrences force complement atomic mass.  That is a genuine dominant-vs-
complement dichotomy not present in the current scalar flower theorem.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only exact full-flower two-blocker and endpoint-degree
localization theorem.  C-II and Level I remain open.
